From: Treasury Solicitor <litigation@tsol.example> (for the Department for Communities and Local Government)
To: Manches LLP <litigation@manches.example> (for E-Nik Ltd)
Date: not stated
Subject: E-Nik Ltd - unpaid invoices of 9 May 2011 and 25 October 2011

Our client's position on the sums claimed is as follows.

1. Clause 2.1 says "based on project requirement". Insofar as the Department's project requirements did not require at least 500 days of consultancy services, there was no obligation to purchase a minimum of 500 days, including after the closure of the Government Offices for the Regions announced in 2010 and effective from the end of March 2011. Further, your client did not invoice up front but invoiced as it went along.
2. There is no provision for pro-rating, so no minimum applies to the period 1 April 2011 to 30 September 2011.
3. Any obligation to purchase arose only upon request. If services were not requested, the claim is in damages, not debt.
4. Your client is put to proof that its consultancy services were made available at all times from 1 April 2009 to 30 September 2011.
5. Clauses 2.1 and 2.3 compel the Department to pay for the minimum amount of the services whether they are supplied or not and are an unenforceable penalty.
6. The £850 per day rate is inclusive of VAT (clause 3.7). VAT cannot be added.
7. Because the invoices wrongly added VAT, there was no "valid invoice" under clause 2.4 and statutory interest under the 1998 Act has not begun to accrue.
8. Alternatively, statutory interest should be remitted under section 5 of the 1998 Act.

--
RECONSTRUCTED FROM THE PUBLISHED JUDGMENT. Not an original document.
